Take a bank account aggregate.

```java
public class Account {
    private String id;
    private long balance;
    private long version;

    private final List<DomainEvent> uncommitted = new ArrayList<>();

    public static Account fromHistory(String id, List<DomainEvent> history) {
        Account a = new Account();
        a.id = id;
        for (DomainEvent e : history) a.apply(e);
        return a;
    }

    public void deposit(long amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        raise(new MoneyDeposited(id, amount, Instant.now()));
    }

    public void withdraw(long amount) {
        if (amount > balance) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        raise(new MoneyWithdrawn(id, amount, Instant.now()));
    }

    private void raise(DomainEvent e) {
        apply(e);
        uncommitted.add(e);
    }

    private void apply(DomainEvent e) {
        switch (e) {
            case MoneyDeposited d -> { balance += d.amount(); version++; }
            case MoneyWithdrawn w -> { balance -= w.amount(); version++; }
        }
    }

    public List<DomainEvent> drainUncommitted() {
        var out = List.copyOf(uncommitted);
        uncommitted.clear();
        return out;
    }
}
```

**Lifecycle:**
1. Load: read all events for `account-123` → `fromHistory(...)` rebuilds state.
2. Command: `account.withdraw(20)` — validates, raises `MoneyWithdrawn` event, applies it.
3. Save: append `drainUncommitted()` to the event store.

**Crucially:** business rules run in the aggregate; only events are persisted. Commands are not stored — only their *outcomes*.
